  journaltitle = {Nature},
  volume       = {592},
  issue        = {7856},
  pages        = {737--746},
  date         = {2021-04},
  doi          = {10.1038/s41586-021-03451-0},
  abstract     = {High-quality and complete reference genome assemblies are
                  fundamental for the application of genomics to biology,
                  disease, and biodiversity conservation. However, such
                  assemblies are available for only a few non-microbial
                  species1-4. To address this issue, the international Genome
                  10K (G10K) consortium5,6 has worked over a five-year period to
                  evaluate and develop cost-effective methods for assembling
                  highly accurate and nearly complete reference genomes. Here we
                  present lessons learned from generating assemblies for 16
                  species that represent six major vertebrate lineages. We
                  confirm that long-read sequencing technologies are essential
                  for maximizing genome quality, and that unresolved complex
