                  Department, Colombia, yielded diverse bat fossils that
                  contribute to the understanding of the approximately 12-13
                  million-year-old La Venta fauna. These newly collected
                  specimens encompass an emballonurid, a noctilionid, three
                  phyllostomids, a thyropterid, three molossids, and a probable
                  vespertilionid. They originate from a stratigraphic level
                  known as the Monkey Beds, dated between 12.5 million and 12.2
                  million years. Noteworthy findings include upper molars of
                  Notonycteris magdalenensis, affirming its close relation to
                  Vampyrum and Chrotopterus, and a novel species of
                  Notonycteris, characterized by smaller size than N.
                  magdalenensis based on two lower molars. Two upper molars from
                  a primitive nectar-feeding bat introduce a new taxon of
