  pages        = {1035--1041},
  date         = {2012-09-01},
  doi          = {10.1111/j.1475-4983.2012.01160.x},
  abstract     = {Among the new dental remains from the late Early Eocene of
                  Chambi (Kasserine area, Tunisia) is a large-sized upper molar
                  of a new bat species, Witwatia sigei nov. sp. (Chi- roptera,
                  Vespertilionoidea, Philisidae), described herein. The locality
                  of Chambi has revealed evidence for an early appear- ance of
                  two modern microchiropteran superfamilies in Africa: Dizzya
                  exsultans, a Philisidae, which is considered to be an archaic
                  Vespertilionoidea, and an indeterminate Rhinolophoi- dea. In
                  addition to D. exsultans, the new species, W. sigei, is the
