  pages        = {e2113628119},
  date         = {2022-04-05},
  doi          = {10.1073/pnas.2113628119},
  abstract     = {SignificanceThe clear need to mitigate zoonotic risk has
                  fueled increased viral discovery in specific reservoir host
                  taxa. We show that a combination of viral and reservoir traits
                  can predict zoonotic virus virulence and transmissibility in
                  humans, supporting the hypothesis that bats harbor
                  exceptionally virulent zoonoses. However, pandemic prevention
                  requires thinking beyond zoonotic capacity, virulence, and
                  transmissibility to consider collective "burden" on human
