  doi          = {10.1073/pnas.1919176117},
  abstract     = {The notion that certain animal groups disproportionately
                  maintain and transmit viruses to humans due to broad-scale
                  differences in ecology, life history, and physiology currently
                  influences global health surveillance and research in disease
                  ecology, virology, and immunology. To directly test whether
                  such "special reservoirs" of zoonoses exist, we used
                  literature searches to construct the largest existing dataset
                  of virus-reservoir relationships, consisting of the avian and
                  mammalian reservoir hosts of 415 RNA and DNA viruses along
                  with their histories of human infection. Reservoir host
                  effects on the propensity of viruses to have been reported as
                  infecting humans were rare and when present were restricted to
                  one or two viral families. The data instead support a largely
                  host-neutral explanation for the distribution of
                  human-infecting viruses across the animal orders studied.
                  After controlling for higher baseline viral richness in
                  mammals versus birds, the observed number of zoonoses per
                  animal order increased as a function of their species
                  richness. Animal orders of established importance as zoonotic
                  reservoirs including bats and rodents were unexceptional,
                  maintaining numbers of zoonoses that closely matched
                  expectations for mammalian groups of their size. Our findings
                  show that variation in the frequency of zoonoses among animal
