  doi          = {10.1038/s41559-024-02485-7},
  abstract     = {Dietary specializations in animals lead to adaptations in
                  morphology, anatomy and physiology. Neotropical bats, with
                  their high taxonomic and trophic diversity, offer a unique
                  perspective on diet-driven evolutionary adaptations. Here we
                  assess the metabolic response to different dietary sugars
                  among wild-caught bats. We found that insectivorous bats had a
                  pronounced metabolic response to trehalose, whereas bats with
                  nectar and fruit-based diets showed significantly higher blood
                  glucose levels in response to glucose and sucrose, reaching
                  levels over 750 mg dl-1. The genomic analysis of 22 focal
                  species and two outgroup species identified positive selection
                  for the digestive enzyme trehalase in insect eaters, while
                  sucrase-isomaltase showed selection in lineages with
                  omnivorous and nectar diets. By examining anatomical and
                  cellular features of the small intestine, we discovered that
                  dietary sugar proportion strongly impacted numerous digestive
                  traits, providing valuable insight into the physiological
                  implications of molecular adaptations. Using hybridization
                  chain reaction (HCR) RNA fluorescence in situ hybridization,
                  we observed unusually high expression in the glucose
                  transporter gene Slc2a2 in nectar bats, while fruit bats
                  increased levels of Slc5a1 and Slc2a5. Overall, this study
                  highlights the intricate interplay between molecular,
                  morphological and physiological aspects of diet evolution,
                  offering new insights into the mechanisms of dietary
                  diversification and sugar assimilation in mammals.},
