  date         = {2022-05-03},
  doi          = {10.1016/j.celrep.2022.110769},
  abstract     = {Distinguishing between conserved and divergent regulatory
                  mechanisms is essential for translating preclinical research
                  from mice to humans, yet there is a lack of information about
                  how evolutionary genome rearrangements affect the regulation
                  of the immune response, a rapidly evolving system. The current
                  model is topologically associating domains (TADs) are
                  conserved between species, buffering evolutionary
                  rearrangements and conserving long-range interactions within a
                  TAD. However, we find that TADs frequently span evolutionary
                  translocation and inversion breakpoints near genes with
                  species-specific expression in immune cells, creating unique
                  enhancer-promoter interactions exclusive to the mouse or human
