  abstract     = {Somatic mutations can have important effects on the life
                  history, ecology, and evolution of plants, but the rate at
                  which they accumulate is poorly understood and difficult to
                  measure directly. Here, we develop a method to measure somatic
                  mutations in individual plants and use it to estimate the
                  somatic mutation rate in a large, long-lived, phenotypically
                  mosaic Eucalyptus melliodora tree. Despite being 100 times
                  larger than Arabidopsis, this tree has a per-generation
                  mutation rate only ten times greater, which suggests that this
                  species may have evolved mechanisms to reduce the mutation
